<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="rosmarinic acid biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5049" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD-25183 &quot;Rosmarinic acid&quot;| (RA) is an ester of |FRAME: CAFFEATE| and |FRAME: CPD-6982|. It is widely spread in species of the |FRAME: TAX-4136| and |FRAME: TAX-21571| |CITS: [12482446]| and also occurs in some lower plants such as ferns and hornworts |CITS: [TAKEDA90]| |CITS: [12721853]|. It was first isolated in 1958 from rosemary (|FRAME: TAX-39367|), which is the source of its name. The 3&apos;-O-&amp;beta;-glucoside of RA (|FRAME:CPD-7018|) has been identified in the hornwort |FRAME: TAX-41834| |CITS: [16133208]|, where it accumulates up to 1% of the cell dry weight. Other rosmarinic acid derivatives of biological importance are |FRAME: CPD-26619 &quot;lithospermic acid&quot;|, a conjugate of rosmarinic acid and caffeic acid, and |FRAME: CPD-26620 &quot;lithospermic acid B&quot;, which is a dimer of rosmarinic acid |CITS: [12482446]|. Rosmarinic acid is used in foods or beverages as a flavoring, in cosmetics, and as a dietary supplement. Its biosynthesis has been studied extensively |CITS: [11943155]|. RA biosynthesis was first investigated in |FRAME: TAX-34256| |CITS: [5484678]| confirming the involvement of two parallel pathways for the making of rosmarinic acid. The metabolic origin for the formation of the two crucial compounds coumaroyl-CoA and 4-hydroxyphenyllactate were identified as L-phenylalanine and L-tyrosine respectively. Further research led to the clarification of the biosynthetic route in |FRAME: TAX-4142| |CITS: [Petersen93]| |CITS: [12482446]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The most experimental support for the RA biosynthesis has been obtained from species of the |FRAME: TAX-4136| family elucidating the detailed enzymatic steps resulting in the formation of RA (|FRAME:PWY-5048|). However, several enzymes of this pathway possess the ability to convert different substrates with comparable efficiency indicating that the production of rosmarinic acid may also be accomplished via a pathway variant (this pathway). Two crucial steps in the RA biosynthetic route comprise the reduction of 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate to 4-hydroxyphenyllactate (hydroxyphenylpyruvate reductase - |CITS: [15284489]|) and the subsequent connection of this RA intermediate with coumaroyl-CoA in a transesterification reaction catalyzed by rosmarinic acid synthase |CITS: [Petersen88]|. In addition to their usual monohydroxylated substrates, both enzymes convert readily the corresponding dihydroxylated substrates to 3,4-dihydroxyphenyllactic acid (DHPL) |CITS: [Hausler91]| |CITS: [15284489]| and rosmarinic acid |CITS: [Petersen91]| respectively. The enzyme catalyzing the entry step into this potential pathway is tyrosine hydroxylase (EC 1.14.16.2) and has been purified from |FRAME: TAX-3583| |CITS: [YAMAMOTO01]|. The formation of L-3,4-dihydroxyphenylalanine (DOPA) can also be accomplished by the action of polyphenol oxidases (EC 1.10.3.1) (e.g. |CITS: [7948897]|). The following reaction (transamination to 3,4-dihydroxypyruvate) has been found to be occurring in animals and bacteria but remains to be validated for plants. It is not known precisely at what stage in the rosmarinic acid biosynthesis the two hydroxygroups in the 3- and 3&apos;-position of the phenolic rings are introduced. At present the depicted pathway can not be ruled out but it is more likely that the major biogenesis of RA proceeds via the pathway described in |FRAME:PWY-5048|.</Description>
